== Thinking like a designer: Creating the aesthetic artifact

The goal when creating philosophy and art is not to think like a player, but to
think like a _designer._ The goal of reading philosophy or experiencing art is
to have your perspective changed. But when creating the experience, you have to
consider your audience's perspective and the best ways to change them. The very
work of aesthetic design is to shake up the reader's worldview, to loosen them
up a little bit. Your goal is to create the rules of play in which the reader
immerses themselves. But the rules do not only encompass rules in the
traditional sense of limitations, but also bare facts: the composition of brush
strokes on a canvas, the delicate movement of a concerto, the precise controls
of a platformer game. These are the restrictions, or in Noë's term,
_resistances,_ through which a free space is opened up for play. Recall our
definition of play as free movement within a restricted plane. Without the
restrictions, there is no play. (Hans agrees on this point.)
